1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,556
Aarav plugs his table
fan into the wall socket.

2
00:00:03,556 --> 00:00:06,207
The voltage there is not steady at all.

3
00:00:06,557 --> 00:00:10,612
It swings from plus to minus and
back, fifty times every second.

4
00:00:10,962 --> 00:00:14,464
So how do we describe a
voltage that never sits still?

5
00:00:14,964 --> 00:00:16,284
With four ideas.

6
00:00:16,284 --> 00:00:19,181
Amplitude, period, frequency and phase.

7
00:00:19,531 --> 00:00:22,160
Let's build them one at
a time, on the scope.

8
00:00:22,810 --> 00:00:26,132
On the breadboard, an AC
source drives a resistor.

9
00:00:26,132 --> 00:00:27,489
Watch the electrons.

10
00:00:27,489 --> 00:00:29,102
They do not flow round.

11
00:00:29,102 --> 00:00:30,810
They rock back and forth.

12
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:35,650
Inside the source, picture an arm
turning in a circle at a steady speed.

13
00:00:35,650 --> 00:00:38,255
Its height at each moment is the voltage.

14
00:00:38,605 --> 00:00:41,354
As the arm turns, the
scope draws its height.

15
00:00:41,354 --> 00:00:43,385
That smooth shape is a sine wave.

16
00:00:44,035 --> 00:00:45,331
First, amplitude.

17
00:00:45,331 --> 00:00:48,767
It is the highest value
the wave reaches, its peak.

18
00:00:48,767 --> 00:00:50,092
We write it V m.

19
00:00:50,442 --> 00:00:53,527
Turn up the amplitude,
and the wave grows taller.

20
00:00:53,527 --> 00:00:55,282
The arm simply gets longer.

21
00:00:55,932 --> 00:00:57,570
Next, the period, T.

22
00:00:57,570 --> 00:01:01,827
It is the time for one complete
cycle, one full turn of the arm.

23
00:01:02,177 --> 00:01:06,255
And the frequency, f, is how
many cycles happen in one second.

24
00:01:06,255 --> 00:01:07,927
It is measured in hertz.

25
00:01:08,277 --> 00:01:10,071
So f equals one over T.

26
00:01:10,071 --> 00:01:14,149
Turn the frequency up, and the
waves squeeze closer together.

27
00:01:14,149 --> 00:01:16,238
The electrons rock faster too.

28
00:01:16,888 --> 00:01:19,390
Now let's write the wave as an equation.

29
00:01:19,740 --> 00:01:24,415
One full turn is three hundred and
sixty degrees, or two pi radians.

30
00:01:25,115 --> 00:01:27,741
The arm makes f turns every second.

31
00:01:27,741 --> 00:01:32,776
So its angular speed is omega equals
two pi f, in radians per second.

32
00:01:33,476 --> 00:01:37,924
After a time t, the arm has
turned through an angle omega t.

33
00:01:37,924 --> 00:01:41,273
Its height is V m times
the sine of omega t.

34
00:01:41,973 --> 00:01:44,874
So small v equals V m sine omega t.

35
00:01:44,874 --> 00:01:47,764
Small v is the value at any instant.

36
00:01:48,414 --> 00:01:50,061
The last idea is phase.

37
00:01:50,061 --> 00:01:54,316
Phase tells you where in its cycle
a wave is, at a given moment.

38
00:01:54,666 --> 00:01:58,955
Now put a second wave on the scope,
with the same frequency, but a quarter

39
00:01:58,955 --> 00:01:59,855
turn ahead.

40
00:02:00,118 --> 00:02:01,655
Its peaks come first.

41
00:02:01,655 --> 00:02:04,020
We say it leads by ninety degrees.

42
00:02:04,020 --> 00:02:06,274
That gap is the phase difference.

43
00:02:06,624 --> 00:02:10,848
We write it as v equals V
m sine of omega t plus phi.

44
00:02:10,848 --> 00:02:12,767
Phi is the phase angle.

45
00:02:13,417 --> 00:02:15,180
Amplitude says how big.

46
00:02:15,180 --> 00:02:17,049
Frequency says how often.

47
00:02:17,049 --> 00:02:18,371
Phase says when.

48
00:02:19,171 --> 00:02:20,326
Worked example.

49
00:02:20,326 --> 00:02:25,370
The mains supply in India is two hundred
and thirty volts, at fifty hertz.

50
00:02:25,720 --> 00:02:27,873
The period is one over fifty.

51
00:02:27,873 --> 00:02:32,002
That is zero point zero two
seconds, or twenty milliseconds.

52
00:02:32,702 --> 00:02:34,792
Omega is two pi times fifty.

53
00:02:34,792 --> 00:02:38,254
About three hundred and
fourteen radians per second.

54
00:02:38,954 --> 00:02:42,071
And two hundred and thirty
volts is not the peak.

55
00:02:42,071 --> 00:02:45,424
The peak is about three
hundred and twenty five volts.

56
00:02:45,424 --> 00:02:47,955
We will see why, in the next episode.

57
00:02:48,605 --> 00:02:50,071
Now, the exam trap.

58
00:02:50,421 --> 00:02:53,149
Many students mix up omega and f.

59
00:02:53,149 --> 00:02:58,699
In v equals V m sine of one hundred
pi t, they say the frequency is one

60
00:02:58,699 --> 00:02:59,781
hundred pi.

61
00:03:00,131 --> 00:03:03,773
One hundred pi is omega,
the angular frequency.

62
00:03:03,773 --> 00:03:05,308
Divide by two pi.

63
00:03:05,308 --> 00:03:07,599
The frequency is fifty hertz.

64
00:03:08,249 --> 00:03:09,922
Where is this asked?

65
00:03:09,922 --> 00:03:15,042
In PUC board papers, as definitions
of frequency, period, amplitude and

66
00:03:15,042 --> 00:03:15,942
phase.

67
00:03:15,727 --> 00:03:19,342
In JEE Main, inside
alternating current problems.

68
00:03:19,992 --> 00:03:20,914
Try this.

69
00:03:20,914 --> 00:03:26,241
An alternating voltage is v equals one
hundred and twenty sine of two hundred

70
00:03:26,241 --> 00:03:27,141
pi t.

71
00:03:26,937 --> 00:03:30,162
Find its peak value, frequency and period.

72
00:03:35,562 --> 00:03:36,462
C.

73
00:03:35,970 --> 00:03:40,555
One hundred and twenty volts, one
hundred hertz, and ten milliseconds.

74
00:03:40,905 --> 00:03:44,069
Compare with v equals V m sine omega t.

75
00:03:44,069 --> 00:03:47,234
So V m is one hundred and twenty volts.

76
00:03:47,584 --> 00:03:49,606
Omega is two hundred pi.

77
00:03:49,606 --> 00:03:53,374
Omega is two pi f, so
f is one hundred hertz.

78
00:03:53,724 --> 00:03:55,231
T is one over f.

79
00:03:55,231 --> 00:03:59,082
One over one hundred is
zero point zero one seconds.

80
00:03:59,082 --> 00:04:00,444
Ten milliseconds.

81
00:04:01,094 --> 00:04:02,615
Screenshot this card.

82
00:04:05,815 --> 00:04:08,119
Next, RMS and average values.

83
00:04:08,119 --> 00:04:12,668
Why the two hundred and thirty volt
mains actually peaks near three hundred

84
00:04:12,668 --> 00:04:13,889
and twenty five.
